{"name":"Evergreen Trail","hex":"#6f7568","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"evergreen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of plants, especially trees, that do not shed their leaves seasonally.","Continually fresh or self-renewing.\n Being a clause which causes an automatic renewal of a contract unless action is taken.\n Of a document, a piece of software, or a data set: kept continually up to date (as opposed to being published at regular intervals and outdated in the meantime)"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub or tree that does not shed its leaves or needles seasonally.","A conifer tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/evergreen"},{"term":"trail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To follow behind (someone or something); to tail (someone or something).","To drag (something) behind on the ground."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The track or indication marking the route followed by something that has passed, such as the footprints of animal on land or the contrail of an airplane in the sky.","A route for travel over land, especially a narrow, unpaved pathway for use by hikers, horseback riders, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Evergreen","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, an evergreen is a plant which has foliage that remains green and functional throughout the year. This contrasts with deciduous plants, which lose their foliage completely during the winter or dry season. Consisting of many different species, the unique feature of evergreen plants lends itself to various environments and purposes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Abies_alba_02.jpg/330px-Abies_alba_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evergreen"},{"title":"Trail","blocks":[{"text":"A trail, also known as a path or track, is a dirt road or a small paved road, usually through an undeveloped natural area, that is designed primarily for use by pedestrians, equestrians and non-motorized vehicles, although occasionally some off-roading motor vehicles may also use it. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, footpath or hiking trail is the preferred term. However, it is sometimes applied to highways in North America. In the United States, the term was historically used to describe the westward expansion trails, which are land routes into or through wildness regions of the American West used by explorers and migrants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg/330px-Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trail"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/%CE%95%CE%B8%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%94%CF%81%CF%85%CE%BC%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A3%CE%B1%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%AC%CF%82.jpg/500px-%CE%95%CE%B8%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%94%CF%81%CF%85%CE%BC%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A3%CE%B1%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%AC%CF%82.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/%CE%95%CE%B8%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%94%CF%81%CF%85%CE%BC%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A3%CE%B1%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%AC%CF%82.jpg/1280px-%CE%95%CE%B8%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%94%CF%81%CF%85%CE%BC%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A3%CE%B1%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%AC%CF%82.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/%CE%95%CE%B8%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%94%CF%81%CF%85%CE%BC%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A3%CE%B1%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%AC%CF%82.jpg/500px-%CE%95%CE%B8%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%94%CF%81%CF%85%CE%BC%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A3%CE%B1%CE%BC%CE%B1%C